Say how far GPU start-up gets, on both sides of the channel.

With Skia on Vulkan in a GPU process of its own, the browser never asked
for a compositor, and in one run the GPU process said nothing after the
Vulkan instance -- not even the device queue it had reported the time
before. A GPU process that has not finished starting and a browser that
never heard it finish look the same from outside, and the process does
not crash, so the shell sees it as settled.

On OHOS each step says so at ERROR: in the GPU process, initialisation
beginning and done (with whether Vulkan and Skia-on-Vulkan survived it),
Vulkan beginning and ready, the context provider and the device queue
being created -- before the call as well as after, so one that never
returns shows -- the service initialised with its host, and each channel
established. In the browser, the GPU process reported initialised, and
each channel asked for and established or failed.

The next run got further: the GPU process initialised with Vulkan and
Skia on it, told the browser so, and every channel after that failed --
the first 0.3 ms after, then again and again, 1280 lines in 25 seconds,
which pushed everything before them out of hilog. So, in the GPU
process, each step after the host is told (channel manager, compositor
GPU thread, the shared context and what Skia runs on), each channel as
the main thread takes it up and, when it fails, why; in the browser,
whether a failed channel was synchronous and whether the GPU service
pipe was still connected -- a synchronous call on a closed pipe fails at
once without the GPU process ever hearing of it.

The repeated lines are thinned to the first five and then every
hundredth, each with its count, and on OHOS a failed channel is asked
for again after a growing pause (100 ms more each time, at most 2 s)
instead of at once. The pause is not diagnostic and stays: a GPU process
that is up and cannot make channels would otherwise be asked dozens of
times a second for as long as the app runs.

None of the GPU process's lines after that point ever reached hilog, the
new ones included: VizMainImpl::CreateGpuService, just before it
initialises the service with its host, replaces the log handler that
wrote to hilog with the one that forwards the log to the browser for
chrome://gpu. The browser now writes what it is forwarded, WARNING and
above, to hilog as "OHOS GPU process says:".

With that, the cause showed: Skia could not make a GrContext on the
2in1 emulator's Vulkan (1.1, translated from the host's GPU), though the
instance, device queue and context provider all came up. It fails only
when the first channel asks for a shared context, long after the GPU
process told the browser it was up, so the process stayed and refused
every channel and the window stayed black.

So on OHOS the GPU service asks Skia for a GrContext once, as soon as the
Vulkan context provider exists and before anything is built on it or the
browser is told what the process can do. If Skia says no, the service
drops the provider, runs Skia on GL, and reports Vulkan disabled; the
browser then records the GPU mode as GL, as for any other fallback. This
part is not diagnostic and stays. GpuServiceImpl's preferences and
feature info stop being const on OHOS for it.

When Skia does fail, the context provider says what it was given: the
instance and device versions, whether it has a memory allocator and
features2, the extensions, and every function Skia's VulkanInterface
requires that the lookup Skia uses cannot find.

The logging is diagnostic; it comes out once Vulkan is understood here.

